## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. The comparison is **referential** (`===`), not deep. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by identity.

```jsx
// Every render creates a NEW object — always fails ===
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a NEW function — always fails ===
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Even if the _contents_ are identical, a new reference means React sees a changed prop and re-renders the child.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A **component** — skips re-render if props didn't change | Child is pure and renders often with stable props |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** — recomputes only when deps change | Value is expensive to compute, or is an object/array passed as a prop |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** — returns same ref when deps unchanged | Function is passed as a prop to a memoized child |

**Rule of thumb:** `useCallback` and `useMemo` are only useful if the thing receiving the value is also memoized. Memoizing in isolation does nothing.

```jsx
// useCallback alone does nothing if Child isn't memoized
const handler = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
<Child onClick={handler} />  // Child still re-renders with parent
```

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context triggers a re-render in **every consumer** when the provider's `value` changes reference.

```jsx
// BAD: new object on every parent render → all consumers re-render
function App() {
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fix: memoize the context value, or split contexts so infrequent data doesn't force re-renders in components that only care about frequent data.

```jsx
// GOOD
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>
```

Another pitfall: putting a large state object in one context. Split into `UserContext` + `UIContext` so a UI state change doesn't re-render user-dependent components.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Parent` renders, even if `items` didn't change:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log('selected', id);

  // New array reference every render
  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.active);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicks: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* ... */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Stable reference — only changes if items changes
  const filtered = useMemo(() => items.filter(i => i.active), [items]);

  // Stable reference — no deps, never recreated
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log('selected', id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicks: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Opt into memoization — skips render if props are referentially equal
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* ... */
});
```

**The triad works together:** `useMemo` stabilizes the value, `useCallback` stabilizes the handler, `React.memo` makes the child actually skip the render. Remove any one piece and the optimization collapses.

---

**When NOT to optimize:** Memoization has a cost (memory, comparison overhead). Don't add it speculatively — profile with React DevTools Profiler first, then target the actual bottlenecks.
